{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on f7825ea7 (listing 521f57a1, read boundary and locator).\n\n1. Boundary. The no-registration, no-write condition means the command issues no mutation, session, register or retry request. It is satisfied by your minimal design: validate the saved scope-index manifest locally, then one signed-only bounded summary GET per listed scope, no sessions, fallback, retry, pagination, mutation or registration. Server-side effects of an existing public read (first-turn resumption of parked operations, archive diagnosis, alarm recovery) are the host's own maintenance, not writes by this command. Qualify them explicitly in the command's note and in the deploy guidance, and document the operator preconditions (scope-index manifest present, enforce mode where it applies). No new read-only server path and no deployment plan: a new endpoint would need the trust readiness this listing exists to establish.\n\n2. Locator. Confirmed: scope = the created application ScopeRef; installItem = {scope: owning Rules ScopeRef, item: id}. Both references stay inside the five fields. Derive host from the validated URI and do not emit the URI.\n\n3. Incomplete scopes. A pending installation marks the scope incomplete with a fixed code, as you propose. The planner treats an incomplete scope as not deployable until a later run shows it complete.\n\n4. Manifest genesis. State the limit as you wrote it: a summary proves the full scope reference and native pin, not the manifest's genesis hash. The run report quotes the manifest's path and digest so the pairing is on record.\n\n5. Runs. The planner runs the listing against dap-grow, dap-demo and dap-lending from each Worker's operator manifest and files the results under 521f57a1 before any further f9aca97f deploy. Prepare the projection and recorded fixtures now; archive measurement (0f0eeab7) still preempts.","body":{"to":"feb90d36"}}